## 6 Monitoring alerts

From the December monitoring pack, items beyond their limits:

1. card Mark I +11.1 points on 2016 (PSI 0.082)
2. auto Mark I +15.3 points on 2016 (PSI 0.092)
3. Mark II-R promise outside the seen interval in 2024Q2
4. 1 new on the commercial watchlist
5. Ballast's 2025 approval ratio F/M 0.957, interval below 1
6. never-paid loans up in branch

For noting: collections now open with a phone call at the first
missed payment, with 10% of accounts given a text first so that the
effect is still measured.

## 7 Model risk

8 tier 1 models in use have no independent validation on file: MK1,
SME1, FRM, PD12, HAZ, LGD, CCF, ECL. The findings log holds 27
findings; 6 high findings are open, and none has a date for its fix:

```
F01  MK1   bureau line not re-set for version 3
F03  MK1   no independent validation since 2015
F09  MK2R  women's 2025 approvals below parity
F13  SME1  owner's bureau line not re-set
F15  FRM   analysts hold grades the model moves
F22  ECL   commercial year-one PD unsettled
```

## 8 The commercial plan, measured

```
supervisor scenario, cumulative         trough  watchlist
today                                    -2.1%      1,079
watchlist headroom cancelled              1.2%        819
watchlist maturities not renewed          1.8%        648
clients cut to 10% of Tier 1              2.0%        648
grades 5-8 repriced                       3.6%        648
watchlist down to its limit               9.5%        283
```

Each step is measured as if complete, on Chapter 26's static book.
The first four are the plan for 2026; with them, Fernhill's own
severe scenario leaves CET1 at 13.5% and economic capital falls from
$820.1m to $629.7m. The fifth, the watchlist run down to its limit,
is the work of 2027.

## 9 Decisions requested

1. Recommend the 2026 risk appetite statement (14 metrics; today 7
   red, 3 amber) to the board in February.
2. Hold CET1 at or above the 16.0% target. Decline the proposal to
   return or lend the $543.1m above the requirement, and make no
   distribution while the supervisor's scenario leaves CET1 below
   8.0%.
3. Approve the watchlist plan: no new money for grades 9-10; undrawn
   revolver headroom cancelled at review; facilities maturing in
   2026 not renewed; watchlist exposure down to $283.3m by December
   2027.
4. Approve a single-client limit of 10% of Tier 1. Allow the 5
   clients above it (Falcon Vale Holdings, Cedar Oak Foods, Willow
   Ember Healthcare, Birch Willow Energy, Alder Harbour Logistics)
   as exceptions until December 2026, to be reduced by
   participations or bought protection.
5. Reprice grades 5-8 at their 2026 reviews (grade 5 +64, grade 6
   +93, grade 7 +188, grade 8 +266 bps), and approve new commercial
   lending only at a margin that earns the 15% hurdle on its
   capital.
6. Let retail lending grow within its limits, and direct the head of
   retail credit to re-set Mark I's bureau points for cards, car
   loans and mortgages (open since July 2019).
7. Approve a validation plan for the 8 tier 1 models in use without
   one, the provision engine and Mark I first, with a date for every
   open high finding.
8. Aim any risk transfer bought in 2026 at the commercial clients
   above the limit, not at the retail book: the car loans, $98.8m of
   exposure, carry $0.5m of the $820.1m of economic capital.

